    "debrief":  {
                    "realTeam":  "In 2008 the Phoenix lander dug a small trench near Mars\u0027s north pole. Its camera saw bright white chunks that slowly disappeared over a few days: water ice turning straight into gas! Phoenix then heated a sample in its oven and confirmed it was water. Today, NASA scientists make maps of where ice hides under the ground, to help choose landing sites for future crews.",
                    "tryNext":  "Go for ice early in the day with high air. Keep the Air maker on overnight so the ice turns into oxygen.",
                    "realScience":  {
                                        "title":  "Making air on Mars",
                                        "text":  "Water can be split into hydrogen and oxygen using electricity. The Perseverance rover carried MOXIE, a small machine that really made oxygen from the Martian air between 2021 and 2023!",
                                        "glossary":  ""
                                    },
